WebSocket
Websocket is a persistent communication protocol over single TCP connection
This basically enables
1. server push
2. real time updates
3. low latency

Problem
Client keeps asking if there is any new data without websocket
with websocket --> new message --> server push --> client 

Advantages
Fast
Efficient 
Not time consuming 
low latency
Persistent --long live


Methods available
@OnOpen -- client connects
@OnMessage -- message received
@OnClose --  client disconnects
@OnError -- error occurs

Scaling Websocket to the enterprise level may be difficult
We will need to use Redis, Kafka , Horizontal Scaling 

Use Cases of websocket
1. Chat Messaging
2. Live cricket score
3. Live dashboard
4. Notifications



Difference between HTTP and WebSocket
Http
1. Unidirectional where cleint send the request and server sends the response 
2. Short Lived
3. Higher latency, due to overhead of new connections setup and headers for each request and response
4. Stateless, handles the request without keeping the track of past interaction 
5. Protocal overhead is high as each request contains the HTTP headers
6. http or https

Websocket
1. BiDirectional -- where client and server can send messages to each other independently
2. Long lived, as it is connected via a single TCP(Trasmission control protocol) connection remains unterminated until it is terminated by either party
3. Low latency -- as the data is transfered over an existing connection wiht minimal overhead
4. Stateful -- server keeps teh track of exisitng connection / active connection enabling easier sessiion management
5. Protocla overhead -- low after initial handshake
6. ws  or wss 



Q. What is web socket handshake
-->Websocket handshake is the initial http process where client and server upgrades to new fully duplex persistent websocket connection using 101 switching protocol response.



